  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 317 AM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\nWater vapor imagery displays large-scale midlevel troughing \nover the eastern CONUS that is associated with the northeastern \ncyclone impacting New England. Even with cyclonic flow occupying\nthe Great Lakes Region, conditions remain quiet today as \nsurface high pressure remains settled over the \nWisconsin/Michigan Border. At the same time, an embedded \nshortwave develops this afternoon over the Northern Rockies and \nprogresses towards the Midwest this week. As this wave lifts \nwith time and remains very moisture starved, some light \nprecipitation will be observed at most locations across the CWA \nthis Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nAfter the wave continues downstream, subsidence aloft returns high \npressure this coming weekend to bring quiet conditions through the \nremainder of the forecast period. Influence from cool, post frontal \nCanadian air will lower temperatures back to climatological norms \nbeyond Thursday with highs in the 60s as we head into October.\n\nForecast Details: \n\nMonday and Tuesday...Even with broad cyclonic flow aloft, dry low \nlevel conditions supported by surface high pressure will keep \nweather rain-free through Tuesday afternoon. 850mb temperatures \naround 10 degrees continue to support seasonably warm temperatures \nwith highs in the 70s this Monday and Tuesday. The only potential \nfor active weather is the continuation of fog in and around marine \nzones due to warm waters warmth and radiational cooling overnight \ncausing dew point depressions to fall. \n\nWednesday and Thursday...The aforementioned wave over the Northern \nRockies will develop surface low pressure over the Central U.S. and \nextend a boundary through the northwoods early Wednesday through \nThursday. Profiles remain relatively dry in the mid and low levels, \nmeaning precipitation will struggle to reach the ground. Model \nguidance continues to indicate medium to high probabilities of total \nrainfall around a quarter inch for most areas of the CWA while our \nwesternmost areas could observe higher amounts where the frontal \nboundary is expected to remain stalled, delivering some appreciable \nmoisture after such a long dry stretch. \n\nFriday through Sunday Outlook... The disheveled midlevel wave is \nexpected to progress downstream Thursday night and return more of a \nzonal flow pattern over the Great Lakes Region this weekend. Surface \nhigh pressure builds over northern Ontario/Manitoba and begins to \ninfluence the CWA with a cooler and dry air mass. No precipitation \nis expected through the remainder of the week with temperatures \nreturning to climatological norms for late October (highs in the \n60s).",
